WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Module 11 closes with the question everyone eventually asks: why did this get expensive, and what actually brings the number down.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.542
Back to the loop from Episode 11: eight tool calls means nine requests, not one.

3
00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:14.723
And the ninth resends everything from the first eight — the whole growing history goes out again on every single lap.

4
00:00:14.723 --> 00:00:18.000
That resend is why agentic tasks cost so much more than a single question.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.985
Where the money actually goes: input tokens.

6
00:00:19.985 --> 00:00:27.789
Global, project, and chat instructions; recalled memory; retrieved knowledge passages; every enabled tool's definition; and the entire conversation history, resent each lap.

7
00:00:27.789 --> 00:00:30.000
The reply you get back is usually the cheap part.

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Ranked, what actually saves money: pick a smaller model for most work, compact long chats, start a fresh chat for a new topic instead of dragging the old one along, enable fewer tools, be specific so the loop stays short, and split large tasks into smaller ones.

9
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.682
What does not help much: shortening your own message, turning memory off, or avoiding tools altogether.

10
00:00:47.682 --> 00:00:53.000
None of those touch the real sinks — instructions, history, and tool definitions dwarf a shorter question every time.

11
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.184
Set caps in Settings, Usage.

12
00:00:55.184 --> 00:01:04.000
Once a cap is reached, the app stops rather than continuing silently — you get a clear halt, not a surprise bill.

13
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.173
Gotcha: the underrated sinks.

14
00:01:05.173 --> 00:01:13.059
Scheduled runs firing unattended, swarms running several conversations at once, a long browser session where every page read gets resent on every following request, and image or video generation.

15
00:01:13.059 --> 00:01:15.000
Watch those before you watch your prompt length.

16
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:15.800
That closes Track B.

17
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:21.000
Track C goes local — starting with the question everyone with real hardware eventually asks: which model actually fits my machine.

